WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.180
In the 1990s, Jim Carrey became one of the
biggest stars in the world, thanks to hit

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.100
films like Ace Ventura: Pet Detective, Dumb
and Dumber, The Mask, and Liar Liar.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.900
He's an incredibly likeable actor, and also
a very loose performer.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.500
He improvises a lot, so when you combine that
with his manic energy, it results in lots

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.890
of unexpected moments of him cracking up his
co-stars … and himself.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.130
Here are some behind-the-scenes clips from
Jim Carrey movies: outtakes so good that they

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.770
could've been in the movies themselves.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.369
Almighty then!

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:35.039
Bruce Almighty is a high-concept blockbuster,
where Carrey stars as a TV reporter who gets

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:37.129
to take a crack at being God.

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:40.960
In this blooper, an agitated Bruce has to
cut off a conversation because he's running

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.229
late for work.

00:00:42.229 --> 00:00:45.589
Generally, actors who have to drive in movies
don't actually drive.

00:00:45.589 --> 00:00:50.269
Instead, the car is placed on a rig so that
the crew can control exactly where it goes.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:53.870
This outtake reveals that secret, as Carrey
fiddles with the controls and pretends to

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.400
zoom out of the neighborhood.

00:01:02.769 --> 00:01:04.520
Poppin' with the penguins

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:09.080
Mr. Popper's Penguins stars Carrey as a guy
who opens up his apartment to dozens of the

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.100
tuxedo-colored polar birds.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.780
For many of the shots, the production used
real penguins, and they've got genuine comic

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:16.780
chops.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.710
In this outtake, Carrey attempts to establish
comic rapport with a prickly penguin, who

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.160
reluctantly obliges.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.870
First, the bird completely ignores his attempts
at friendliness.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.500
Then, when he attempts the old "it's for you"
bit with a phone, the little critter amusingly

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.550
snaps its beak as a clever retort.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.390
"I'll introduce you.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.850
Somebody wants to talk."

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.920
"Wanna say 'hi' to Pippy?"

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.150
"Whoa, yeah!"

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.490
Water in the court

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.170
In Liar Liar, Carrey plays a sleazy lawyer
who is physically unable to say anything but

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.170
the truth.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.600
Ironically enough, in this outtake, the joke
is based on a lie.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.010
After an exchange with co-star Jennifer Tilly,
Carrey pours himself a glass of water from

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.010
a pitcher.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.920
He knows that neither the glass nor the pitcher
is going to appear on screen … but that

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.810
the sound WILL register.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.340
So it's the perfect opportunity for Carrey
to make it seem like he's relieving himself

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.710
in the courtroom.

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:16.870
"Ahhhhh."

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.840
Something About Mary Christmas

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.659
In this moment from Dumb and Dumber To, best
friends Harry Dunne and Lloyd Christmas are

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:24.620
having one of their little arguments.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.829
In the outtake, Carrey adds some highly theatrical
laughing.

00:02:27.829 --> 00:02:33.120
His co-star Jeff Daniels doesn't break character,
instead managing to improvise this line:

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.750
"Yeah, yeah, yeah!"

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.600
"Let me just laugh some more!"

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.080
It's a joke that works on two levels, referencing
both the name of Lloyd's unattainable dream

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:49.489
woman from the original Dumb and Dumber, as
well as another very successful Farrelly brothers

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:50.529
film.

00:02:50.529 --> 00:02:52.620
Hello, Clarice

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.849
Carrey departed from his wheelhouse of likeable
idiots in the 1996 dark comedy The Cable Guy.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:02.239
He plays a TV installer who befriends a regular
guy and then stalks him and tries to ruin

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:03.299
his life.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:06.659
One of the movie's most famous scenes takes
place at Medieval Times.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:09.999
Carrey puts a bunch of meat on his face and
references Hannibal Lecter from The Silence

00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:11.320
of the Lambs.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.150
It was apparently so funny to Carrey and Matthew
Broderick that they struggled to film the

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.540
scene without cracking up.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.040
"Silence.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.460
OF THE LAMBS!"

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.200
"Stop that, that is incredibly disgusting."

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.240
"Do I have a lot of grease on my face?"

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.240
"Yes!"

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:26.860
Fun with Jim and props

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.010
Jim Carrey knows a thing or two about physical
comedy, but that doesn't necessarily mean

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.540
he's the most physically coordinated actor.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:39.200
In this outtake from Fun with Dick and Jane,
Carrey's suburban dad-turned-criminal is supposed

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.930
to celebrate a successful phone call by throwing
the phone from one hand to the other.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.129
But he can't quite seem to pull it off, as
he drops the phone and sends it flying.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:50.919
When he actually does manage to catch it,
he pauses to savor the moment, but for so

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.140
long that the take is unusable.

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:04.379
A series of fortunate riffs

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:08.439
A Series of Unfortunate Events is based on
Lemony Snicket's gothic children's novels

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:10.700
about the orphaned Baudelaire children.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.129
With Carrey as villainous master of disguise
Count Olaf, there ended up being plenty of

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:16.510
extra footage.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.590
In this clip, he improvises a song about a
time machine.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:24.060
That leads to him imitating a clock gone mad,
which then leads to his top-notch dinosaur

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.200
impression.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.250
Somehow his co-stars continue to pretend to
sleep, even as he runs around and, well, does

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:30.760
this:

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:36.720
"Ahhhhhh!"

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.800
What's my name again?

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.620
Anchorman 2 did everything the first Anchorman
did, only much bigger and set in the '80s

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.010
instead of the '70s.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.060
This included revisiting the first film's
epic news anchor battle royale.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:56.370
The original fight featured only local San
Diego news teams while the second increased

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.460
the stakes with national and international
squads.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.200
There were cameos galore, including Carrey
and Marion Cotillard as a Canadian duo.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:08.160
Though Carrey is a native Canuck, his character's
exaggerated accent made it hard for him to

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.220
spit out his lines.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.970
"What the hell's my name?"

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:11.970
"Scott Riley."

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:12.970
"Scott Riley."

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.591
"There's not going to be a fight, without
Ron… what's my name?

00:05:15.591 --> 00:05:16.591
My name is not Ron!"

